1
00:00:01,083 --> 00:00:02,168
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:02,293 --> 00:00:05,546
- You really like this guy, huh?
- He got a promotion in D.C.

3
00:00:05,713 --> 00:00:08,674
and he asked me to go with him
so we can be together.

4
00:00:09,592 --> 00:00:12,303
My sister's Amy Welker.
You have to find her.

5
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:14,222
- She was abducted with you?
- Yes.

6
00:00:14,347 --> 00:00:16,057
"How long were you held captive?"

7
00:00:16,265 --> 00:00:18,851
"I couldn't tell.
I never saw the sunlight."

8
00:00:19,018 --> 00:00:20,353
"Two days, maybe."

9
00:00:20,478 --> 00:00:22,230
Her sister was there.
There are more girls.

10
00:00:22,396 --> 00:00:23,606
- You're right-handed.
- Yes.

11
00:00:23,856 --> 00:00:25,900
So, that heart on your hand,
y... you didn't draw that?

12
00:00:26,025 --> 00:00:27,109
No. They did.

13
00:00:27,485 --> 00:00:30,154
They took a sample of blood from all the
trafficking victims, including Daniela.

14
00:00:30,321 --> 00:00:32,281
- Why do you think they needed the blood?
- I don't know yet.

15
00:00:32,406 --> 00:00:35,076
Look, I... I've been thinking
about you leaving.

16
00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:37,703
And I really want you
to be happy.

17
00:00:38,079 --> 00:00:39,830
And that is the most important
thing to me...

18
00:00:39,956 --> 00:00:41,832
That you do
what makes you happy.

19
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:50,466
The body's been cut open.

20
00:00:50,758 --> 00:00:52,635
They're killing these girls
for their organs.

21
00:00:54,971 --> 00:00:57,139
And I need a list of every
transplant facility in the country.

22
00:00:57,431 --> 00:00:59,141
We need to track down
where these organs went.

23
00:00:59,976 --> 00:01:01,018
Thanks, Wylie.

24
00:01:01,143 --> 00:01:03,604
What's going on?
The office said multiple homicides?

25
00:01:03,771 --> 00:01:05,064
Three, all women.

26
00:01:05,481 --> 00:01:07,900
And we believe they were
from the same trafficking operation

27
00:01:08,025 --> 00:01:09,277
that we investigated.

28
00:01:09,652 --> 00:01:10,695
It gets worse.

29
00:01:10,861 --> 00:01:12,488
All three girls were missing vital organs.

30
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:14,991
- What?
- They were harvested...

31
00:01:15,116 --> 00:01:16,617
We think for sale on the black market.

32
00:01:17,827 --> 00:01:18,995
How can I help?

33
00:01:19,287 --> 00:01:21,414
You could start with this place...
Wheatondale Medical Center.

34
00:01:22,164 --> 00:01:24,709
We need to know who had access to it.
And, also, the Qasimi brothers.

35
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:26,586
Well, you don't think they had anything
to do with this?

36
00:01:26,794 --> 00:01:28,713
They worked closely
with whoever abducted these girls.

37
00:01:28,963 --> 00:01:31,007
I'm guessing the same people
are behind their murders.

38
00:01:31,173 --> 00:01:32,967
I'll contact Beaumont penitentiary.

39
00:01:33,217 --> 00:01:35,052
I should be able
to see the Qasimis tonight.

40
00:01:35,177 --> 00:01:37,263
Okay, if you guys have
any problems, give me a call.

41
00:01:58,659 --> 00:01:59,869
Three women.

42
00:02:00,369 --> 00:02:01,662
Think there were more?

43
00:02:01,871 --> 00:02:02,997
Many more.

44
00:02:05,041 --> 00:02:07,001
Covered up like they're old cars
just...

45
00:02:07,877 --> 00:02:10,588
set there unceremoniously.

46
00:02:10,713 --> 00:02:13,132
It's like you set down a cup of tea.

47
00:02:13,341 --> 00:02:16,469
And look at their feet...
Their blood types written on their soles.

48
00:02:16,594 --> 00:02:18,179
There's nothing special
about these murders.

49
00:02:18,304 --> 00:02:21,182
It's just routine, transactional.

50
00:02:22,099 --> 00:02:24,560
It's a business, a production line.

51
00:02:25,186 --> 00:02:26,354
Exactly.

52
00:02:29,523 --> 00:02:32,568
"Our bureau's major-crimes unit
covers the greater D.C. area."

53
00:02:32,693 --> 00:02:34,362
"We investigate hundreds
of crimes each year"

54
00:02:34,487 --> 00:02:37,531
"ranging from homicide
to armed robbery to kidnapping."

55
00:02:37,657 --> 00:02:40,368
- Sounds like you've got your hands full.
- "We do."

56
00:02:40,493 --> 00:02:42,787
"So when Pike told me you were thinking
of making a move to D.C."

57
00:02:42,912 --> 00:02:45,873
"I jumped at the chance to snap you up
before someone else did."

58
00:02:46,415 --> 00:02:48,584
"Job's yours if you want it."

59
00:02:50,044 --> 00:02:53,005
Uh, that sounds great, sir.
I just need some time to think about it.

60
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:55,007
"Wouldn't have it any other way.
But do me a favor."

61
00:02:55,383 --> 00:02:56,801
"Let me know as soon as you can."

62
00:02:57,051 --> 00:02:58,386
- Thank you.
- "Thank you."

63
00:02:58,511 --> 00:03:01,138
- "Pike, Abbott."
- Talk soon, Don.

64
00:03:02,181 --> 00:03:03,224
Wow.

65
00:03:03,516 --> 00:03:05,267
Sounds like a great gig, Teresa.

66
00:03:06,185 --> 00:03:07,853
You know we'd be sorry to lose you.

67
00:03:11,649 --> 00:03:12,983
So...

68
00:03:14,485 --> 00:03:15,695
What are you thinking?

69
00:03:16,821 --> 00:03:19,865
- I don't know yet.
- Well, Abbott's right. It's a great job.

70
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:22,284
- And Don's the best.
- I believe you.

71
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:28,874
Well, look, Teresa.
I've been patient.

72
00:03:29,125 --> 00:03:30,167
I...

73
00:03:30,876 --> 00:03:33,087
I kinda went out on a limb for you...

74
00:03:33,421 --> 00:03:37,383
Pushed Don to pass on other candidates
so that I could have you with me in D.C.

75
00:03:38,217 --> 00:03:41,053
I mean, I know it's a big decision
but it's a decision you need to make.

76
00:03:41,178 --> 00:03:42,221
Morning, Lisbon.

77
00:03:43,848 --> 00:03:44,890
Pike.

78
00:03:58,610 --> 00:04:00,112
All right, got anything?

79
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:03,699
We might have a hit on the truck used to
traffic the girls to the Qasimi brothers.

80
00:04:03,824 --> 00:04:04,867
It's the one we intercepted.

81
00:04:05,534 --> 00:04:07,786
We've had that truck for days.
What's taking so long?

82
00:04:08,036 --> 00:04:11,373
Well, the truck was leased by one company
which is owned by another company

83
00:04:11,498 --> 00:04:15,711
which is a subsidiary of another company.
There's six in total, all privately owned.

84
00:04:15,961 --> 00:04:17,963
Which makes figuring out
who owns it a nightmare.

85
00:04:18,505 --> 00:04:19,965
I just got back from the prison.

86
00:04:20,215 --> 00:04:22,426
- The Qasimi brothers are dead.
- What?

87
00:04:22,551 --> 00:04:24,428
{\an8}They were stabbed to death
in their cell this morning.

88
00:04:25,512 --> 00:04:27,681
{\an8}Whoever abducted these girls
must have gotten to them.

89
00:04:36,523 --> 00:04:37,775
{\an8}We think Amy is with a group of girls

90
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,860
{\an8}that was trafficked
across the border into Mexico.

91
00:04:40,944 --> 00:04:43,197
{\an8}From there, she would've been
transported by cargo ship

92
00:04:43,906 --> 00:04:45,866
{\an8}most likely to a buyer in South America.

93
00:04:46,116 --> 00:04:48,577
- South America?
- We estimate Amy will be arriving

94
00:04:48,702 --> 00:04:50,996
{\an8}at a foreign port
in the next few days.

95
00:04:51,121 --> 00:04:52,664
{\an8}That'll be our best chance to find her.

96
00:04:53,957 --> 00:04:57,294
{\an8}Why wasn't I with her?
Why was I held separately?

97
00:04:57,461 --> 00:04:59,630
{\an8}A small number of girls
were pulled from the main group.

98
00:05:00,047 --> 00:05:01,131
You were one of them.

99
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:03,717
That's why you had the heart
drawn on your hand.

100
00:05:06,303 --> 00:05:07,513
{\an8}What happened to them?

101
00:05:09,890 --> 00:05:11,642
{\an8}They were taken
to an abandoned medical facility

102
00:05:11,767 --> 00:05:13,018
outside Dallas.

103
00:05:14,311 --> 00:05:15,562
{\an8}We found the girls murdered.

104
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:17,940
Their organs had been harvested.

105
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:21,610
{\an8}Oh, my God.

106
00:05:23,487 --> 00:05:25,906
{\an8}- Was... was Amy...
- No.

107
00:05:26,323 --> 00:05:27,449
{\an8}We're sure of that.

108
00:05:29,326 --> 00:05:30,452
We're gonna find her.

109
00:05:43,173 --> 00:05:44,216
What you doin'?

110
00:05:44,633 --> 00:05:47,261
{\an8}Nothing'. Thinking.

111
00:05:47,386 --> 00:05:50,472
{\an8}What, uh, meaning-of-life-type stuff?

112
00:05:51,139 --> 00:05:52,808
{\an8}Something like that, yeah.

113
00:05:53,892 --> 00:05:56,478
{\an8}I got something.
Wheatondale Medical Center.

114
00:05:56,603 --> 00:05:58,689
{\an8}I started background checks
on everyone associated with it.

115
00:05:58,814 --> 00:05:59,940
- Anything turn up?
- No.

116
00:06:00,065 --> 00:06:01,859
{\an8}But then I cross-referenced
that list of names

117
00:06:01,984 --> 00:06:03,068
{\an8}with the trucking-company information.

118
00:06:03,402 --> 00:06:04,987
{\an8}Got a hit...
Michael Ridley.

119
00:06:05,529 --> 00:06:07,406
{\an8}- Who's he?
- A partner in the equity group

120
00:06:07,531 --> 00:06:08,991
that sold Wheatondale two years ago

121
00:06:09,116 --> 00:06:11,034
so he would've had access to the building.

122
00:06:11,159 --> 00:06:14,037
{\an8}And he just so happens to own
the same company

123
00:06:14,162 --> 00:06:16,081
I traced our confiscated truck back to.

124
00:06:23,088 --> 00:06:24,673
{\an8}That is quite a coincidence.

125
00:06:24,798 --> 00:06:25,883
{\an8}Yes, it is.

126
00:06:26,008 --> 00:06:28,051
{\an8}So, you can understand
why we're here, Mr. Ridley.

127
00:06:28,302 --> 00:06:30,804
{\an8}Oh, I'll admit it's suspect
but I can assure you I had nothing to do

128
00:06:30,929 --> 00:06:32,097
{\an8}with these horrific acts.

129
00:06:32,764 --> 00:06:35,183
What was your involvement
in the Wheatondale Medical Center?

130
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:38,061
{\an8}It was a distressed property.
My partners and I purchased it

131
00:06:38,186 --> 00:06:40,564
{\an8}sold off the medical equipment
that was still valuable.

132
00:06:40,731 --> 00:06:42,274
Just like harvesting a crop.

133
00:06:42,482 --> 00:06:44,067
When was the last time you were there?

134
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:47,446
Wheatondale? I've never been there.

135
00:06:48,071 --> 00:06:50,449
{\an8}- Mr. Ridley, what was...
- Interesting office.

136
00:06:51,199 --> 00:06:52,993
{\an8}Very plain, simple.

137
00:06:53,744 --> 00:06:55,245
{\an8}No muss, no fuss.

138
00:06:56,496 --> 00:06:58,040
{\an8}Never felt the need to decorate.

139
00:06:58,415 --> 00:06:59,791
{\an8}An office is for work.

140
00:07:00,334 --> 00:07:01,376
{\an8}You were saying?

141
00:07:01,501 --> 00:07:05,255
{\an8}What were 30 abducted women doing
in a truck owned by your company?

142
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:07,966
One of my companies.
I'm in shipping and logistics.

143
00:07:08,091 --> 00:07:11,511
{\an8}- I own or co-own dozens.
- Let's talk about that.

144
00:07:11,678 --> 00:07:14,097
{\an8}Your corporate structure
is incredibly convoluted.

145
00:07:14,556 --> 00:07:17,809
It's almost like you didn't want the truck
linked back to your company.

146
00:07:17,935 --> 00:07:19,019
Not at all.

147
00:07:19,144 --> 00:07:20,729
It's simply done that way
for tax purposes.

148
00:07:20,854 --> 00:07:22,314
I wouldn't wanna bore you with the details.

149
00:07:22,439 --> 00:07:24,149
What is so boring about the details?

150
00:07:24,316 --> 00:07:26,818
Well, to me, nothing, but for most people

151
00:07:26,944 --> 00:07:28,987
corporate accounting isn't exactly thrilling.

152
00:07:29,112 --> 00:07:30,572
- We've got time.
- Unless, of course

153
00:07:30,697 --> 00:07:32,157
there's something you're trying to hide.

154
00:07:35,243 --> 00:07:37,037
I think we're getting off
on the wrong foot.

155
00:07:38,497 --> 00:07:40,457
Here's the name and number
of my CFO.

156
00:07:42,834 --> 00:07:45,003
He'll make sure you have access
to everything you need.

157
00:07:46,797 --> 00:07:48,507
I have absolutely nothing to hide.

158
00:08:01,144 --> 00:08:02,354
Hello, Mr. Ridley.

159
00:08:02,854 --> 00:08:05,315
Anthony, I just had a conversation
with the FBI.

160
00:08:05,816 --> 00:08:09,152
- I see. Is everything all right?
- No.

161
00:08:09,653 --> 00:08:10,946
Apparently, they found three dead women

162
00:08:11,071 --> 00:08:12,781
at the Wheatondale medical facility.

163
00:08:13,991 --> 00:08:17,452
Well, that is disturbing.
Is there anything I can do to help?

164
00:08:18,078 --> 00:08:19,997
I'm sure the FBI will find
whoever's responsible.

165
00:08:20,122 --> 00:08:21,832
Nevertheless, I'd like you to look into it.

166
00:08:21,957 --> 00:08:24,376
Absolutely, sir.
Anything you need.

167
00:08:30,215 --> 00:08:31,883
- Hey, Cho.
- Hey.

168
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:34,302
You got a minute?

169
00:08:34,803 --> 00:08:35,846
What's up?

170
00:08:36,304 --> 00:08:39,474
I got offered a job in D.C.

171
00:08:41,852 --> 00:08:42,978
You gonna take it?

172
00:08:43,103 --> 00:08:44,730
I don't know. Maybe.

173
00:08:45,397 --> 00:08:47,441
- Don't tell anybody, all right?
- Okay.

174
00:08:47,858 --> 00:08:49,109
It'd be a great move, though.

175
00:08:49,651 --> 00:08:50,861
- You think?
- Absolutely.

176
00:08:51,653 --> 00:08:53,280
You remember the first day
we worked together?

177
00:08:54,072 --> 00:08:55,365
- Sure.
- I almost quit.

178
00:08:56,158 --> 00:08:57,534
- Really? Why?
- Rigsby.

179
00:08:58,660 --> 00:09:01,455
But then I saw the way you worked.
I knew I had to stay.

180
00:09:02,497 --> 00:09:04,458
I wouldn't be where I am today
if it weren't for you.

181
00:09:06,043 --> 00:09:07,794
Whatever decision you make,
it's been an honor.

182
00:09:09,046 --> 00:09:10,088
Thank you.

183
00:09:17,929 --> 00:09:19,556
By the way, they're ordering
Chinese for lunch.

184
00:09:26,605 --> 00:09:28,648
Forensics found something
at Wheatondale...

185
00:09:28,774 --> 00:09:30,567
DNA from 16 different people, so far.

186
00:09:31,193 --> 00:09:33,570
Wait. Are you saying that 16 women
were killed in there?

187
00:09:33,695 --> 00:09:36,740
About 15 of them were women.
Some of the DNA came from a man.

188
00:09:36,865 --> 00:09:38,241
His name's Alexander Lark.

189
00:09:38,408 --> 00:09:40,410
At first, I thought
he might be another victim

190
00:09:40,535 --> 00:09:42,037
but he was never reported missing.

191
00:09:42,162 --> 00:09:43,371
Do you think he was involved?

192
00:09:43,497 --> 00:09:45,332
I think that he's the one
that carved up these women.

193
00:09:45,874 --> 00:09:47,834
Up until seven years ago,
Lark was a surgeon

194
00:09:47,959 --> 00:09:49,503
and that's when he lost his license.

195
00:09:49,795 --> 00:09:50,879
Well, where is he now?

196
00:09:51,213 --> 00:09:53,965
The local P.D. went to Lark's house
but they didn't find anything.

197
00:09:54,216 --> 00:09:56,343
His neighbors haven't seen him
in a couple of days.

198
00:09:56,718 --> 00:09:58,345
Does he have any family in the Area?

199
00:09:58,553 --> 00:10:00,806
He has a daughter, April.
She lives in San Antonio.

200
00:10:00,972 --> 00:10:04,309
Okay. Well, grab Cho and see
what the daughter knows.

201
00:10:08,438 --> 00:10:10,148
April, have you spoken
to your father recently?

202
00:10:10,357 --> 00:10:11,566
Does two years ago count?

203
00:10:11,858 --> 00:10:15,070
He's been missing for a few days.
We need to ask him some questions.

204
00:10:15,654 --> 00:10:17,155
Like I said, we don't talk much.

205
00:10:18,073 --> 00:10:20,617
- Why not?
- Do you have a couple hours?

206
00:10:23,078 --> 00:10:25,372
Mom died, I don't know, eight years ago.

207
00:10:25,497 --> 00:10:27,541
Dad decided that was a good time
to start drinking.

208
00:10:27,833 --> 00:10:29,292
He became someone different...

209
00:10:29,751 --> 00:10:31,378
Lost his job, his house.

210
00:10:31,670 --> 00:10:33,630
I don't even know
how to be around him anymore.

211
00:10:33,755 --> 00:10:35,215
Do you have any guesses
where he might have gone?

212
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:36,675
Anything could be useful.

213
00:10:37,509 --> 00:10:40,137
It's probably nothing
but my father grew up near the beach

214
00:10:40,262 --> 00:10:41,638
in Corpus Christi.

215
00:10:42,264 --> 00:10:44,432
Sometimes, when he wanted
to get away, he'd go there.

216
00:10:45,392 --> 00:10:46,810
Okay. Thanks for your time.

217
00:10:47,936 --> 00:10:50,147
What kinda trouble is he in, anyway?

218
00:10:50,438 --> 00:10:52,440
Actually, never mind.
I don't even wanna know.

219
00:11:29,311 --> 00:11:31,229
Thank you for seeing us
again, Mr. Ridley.

220
00:11:31,354 --> 00:11:32,439
Anything to help.

221
00:11:33,732 --> 00:11:36,568
- Garish, I know. My ex-wife's idea.
- No.

222
00:11:37,319 --> 00:11:39,487
It was your idea.
It's an intimidation technique.

223
00:11:39,779 --> 00:11:41,489
- Is it?
- Yeah. You want whoever comes here

224
00:11:41,615 --> 00:11:43,825
to know exactly who they're dealing with.
Well...

225
00:11:44,409 --> 00:11:46,411
- now we know.
- Very perceptive.

226
00:11:47,287 --> 00:11:48,455
So, how can I help you?

227
00:11:48,663 --> 00:11:49,998
You recognize this man?

228
00:11:50,999 --> 00:11:52,834
His name's Alexander Lark.

229
00:11:53,043 --> 00:11:54,502
He was a doctor years ago.

230
00:11:54,669 --> 00:11:56,171
No, can't say that I do.

231
00:11:56,296 --> 00:11:57,547
- He's lying.
- Excuse me?

232
00:11:57,672 --> 00:12:00,800
Lark worked with you. It's as plain
to me as that nose on your face.

233
00:12:01,134 --> 00:12:03,220
I'm beginning to realize
you've got a certain idea about me

234
00:12:03,345 --> 00:12:06,681
but let me be more than clear.
You are mistaken.

235
00:12:06,806 --> 00:12:08,683
We're gonna find Lark,
and when we do

236
00:12:08,808 --> 00:12:10,310
we'll know if you're involved.

237
00:12:12,604 --> 00:12:13,939
"Cho, it's Wylie."

238
00:12:14,189 --> 00:12:16,274
"Corpus Christi P.D. spotted Lark's car."

239
00:12:16,441 --> 00:12:17,651
"It's in the Marina district."

240
00:12:18,318 --> 00:12:19,486
There's his car.

241
00:12:24,282 --> 00:12:25,325
Here we go.

242
00:12:28,328 --> 00:12:29,412
Alexander Lark!

243
00:12:29,663 --> 00:12:32,457
- FBI! Don't even think about running!
- Let me see your hands.

244
00:12:33,250 --> 00:12:35,669
Just relax. Calm down.

245
00:12:36,211 --> 00:12:38,630
"Hello, Anthony. What's the status?"

246
00:12:39,339 --> 00:12:42,175
We have a problem.
The FBI is arresting Lark.

247
00:12:42,425 --> 00:12:44,219
They cannot take him alive.

248
00:12:48,098 --> 00:12:49,140
All right, let's go.

249
00:12:49,391 --> 00:12:50,558
"Is Lark in range?"

250
00:12:50,725 --> 00:12:52,978
Yes, but he's not alone.

251
00:12:53,103 --> 00:12:54,938
Go. Go now.

252
00:13:12,205 --> 00:13:14,457
- Everybody okay?
- Yeah, I think so.

253
00:13:38,313 --> 00:13:39,480
You've had your drink.

254
00:13:39,897 --> 00:13:42,108
Now tell us how Ridley's
operation worked.

255
00:13:43,359 --> 00:13:44,402
Uh...

256
00:13:45,069 --> 00:13:47,071
Ridley had a guy named Jesse.

257
00:13:48,406 --> 00:13:51,659
His real name was James O'Conner.
He was found dead this week.

258
00:13:53,119 --> 00:13:56,539
Uh, h... he'd find the girls for Ridley.

259
00:13:56,664 --> 00:13:57,874
"Most of them were sold off"

260
00:13:58,082 --> 00:14:00,793
"but if their tissue and blood type
were a match"

261
00:14:00,918 --> 00:14:02,795
"for someone needing
an organ transplant"

262
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:06,257
"they were brought to me...
a heart drawn on their hand."

263
00:14:06,466 --> 00:14:07,508
"And you killed them?"

264
00:14:07,967 --> 00:14:10,428
"They came to me drugged."

265
00:14:12,013 --> 00:14:14,098
But yes, yes.

266
00:14:14,724 --> 00:14:16,059
Propofol.

267
00:14:16,642 --> 00:14:18,061
They never felt a thing.

268
00:14:20,104 --> 00:14:24,525
I'd remove the organ and one
of Ridley's employees would pick it up

269
00:14:24,650 --> 00:14:26,694
"and deliver it to the buyer."

270
00:14:26,861 --> 00:14:32,575
Ridley forged paperwork
so the organs seemed aboveboard.

271
00:14:33,076 --> 00:14:36,204
The hospitals that performed
the transplants never had a clue.

272
00:14:36,537 --> 00:14:38,790
This employee...
you have a name?

273
00:14:39,207 --> 00:14:42,585
No. He wasn't a chatty type.

274
00:14:43,044 --> 00:14:47,173
"He was, uh, young, maybe late 20s."

275
00:14:47,465 --> 00:14:50,051
"I'd store the bodies in the freezer"

276
00:14:50,176 --> 00:14:54,138
and every few weeks, that same guy
would come and take them away.

277
00:14:55,973 --> 00:14:57,892
When did you start working for Ridley?

278
00:14:58,601 --> 00:15:01,312
Uh... six years ago.

279
00:15:02,021 --> 00:15:03,564
He was an old patient.

280
00:15:03,898 --> 00:15:08,194
When I lost my license,
he offered me a job.

281
00:15:08,611 --> 00:15:12,323
I needed the money, and he paid well.

282
00:15:13,032 --> 00:15:18,246
You see, Ridley's been involved
with human trafficking for years.

283
00:15:18,371 --> 00:15:22,041
He used the money he made
to go into legitimate business.

284
00:15:22,708 --> 00:15:23,918
I'm curious.

285
00:15:25,086 --> 00:15:28,548
Did it ever bother you...
killing these women?

286
00:15:31,634 --> 00:15:32,677
Sure.

287
00:15:33,010 --> 00:15:34,095
Of course.

288
00:15:35,763 --> 00:15:37,390
But after a while...

289
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:41,060
you stop thinking about it.

290
00:15:48,317 --> 00:15:50,194
And that means profits for the next quarter

291
00:15:50,319 --> 00:15:52,530
are expected to rise by at least 7%.

292
00:15:52,655 --> 00:15:54,031
- Yeah, I wouldn't be so sure of that
- Perhaps more.

293
00:15:54,157 --> 00:15:56,117
especially when your CEO
is behind bars.

294
00:15:56,242 --> 00:15:59,370
Michael Ridley, you're under arrest
for human trafficking and murder.

295
00:15:59,495 --> 00:16:00,955
You have the right to remain silent.

296
00:16:01,080 --> 00:16:04,834
Anything you say or do can and will be
used against you in a court of law.

297
00:16:08,045 --> 00:16:10,214
Ridley's not talking so we don't know
which ship the girls are on

298
00:16:10,339 --> 00:16:12,049
but we believe
they're headed to South America.

299
00:16:12,258 --> 00:16:13,426
"How many ships are we looking at?"

300
00:16:13,551 --> 00:16:15,803
45 total, but 33 have docked
and been inspected.

301
00:16:15,928 --> 00:16:17,472
Unfortunately, nothing was found.

302
00:16:17,805 --> 00:16:20,308
The remaining 12 will be arriving
in your territories.

303
00:16:20,433 --> 00:16:21,559
We need a search of each one.

304
00:16:21,726 --> 00:16:23,269
"That's gonna be a problem on my end."

305
00:16:23,478 --> 00:16:25,730
"Colombian port officials
are bought off by the cartels"

306
00:16:25,855 --> 00:16:27,106
"and drugs are going in and out."

307
00:16:27,398 --> 00:16:29,317
"They're not just gonna
open the doors for the FBI."

308
00:16:33,988 --> 00:16:36,240
{\an8}"Ambassador Moreno,
this is Agent Cho with the FBI."

309
00:16:36,491 --> 00:16:39,577
{\an8}Hello, agent. I was just briefed
on your situation.

310
00:16:40,161 --> 00:16:42,205
I'd be happy to facilitate
a search warrant

311
00:16:42,371 --> 00:16:43,414
"but it may take some time."

312
00:16:43,581 --> 00:16:44,707
We don't have time, sir.

313
00:16:44,874 --> 00:16:46,584
I'm sorry, but there's a chain
of procedure.

314
00:16:46,834 --> 00:16:49,128
You understand what will happen
to these girls if we don't find them?

315
00:16:49,504 --> 00:16:51,464
- "Agent Cho..."
- You understand what will happen to you?

316
00:16:52,673 --> 00:16:54,634
- Are you threatening me?
- No, sir.

317
00:16:54,759 --> 00:16:56,344
But I'm not an enemy
you wanna make.

318
00:16:57,220 --> 00:16:59,388
Now, we know that the cartels
have infiltrated local government...

319
00:16:59,514 --> 00:17:01,474
Even high-ranking officials like yourself.

320
00:17:01,641 --> 00:17:03,601
"If this warrant doesn't come through
in time"

321
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:05,311
"we'll be forced to look into it."

322
00:17:08,481 --> 00:17:09,690
Let me see what I can do.

323
00:17:10,441 --> 00:17:11,484
Thank you, sir.

324
00:17:13,236 --> 00:17:14,779
They're letting Ridley out on bail.

325
00:17:25,206 --> 00:17:28,709
I'd say congratulations
but I think your victory will be short-lived.

326
00:17:29,460 --> 00:17:31,754
- Give us a moment?
- Michael. I...

327
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:39,053
I know you want me punished.

328
00:17:39,512 --> 00:17:40,805
You wanna see me suffer.

329
00:17:41,138 --> 00:17:42,473
I wouldn't object to it.

330
00:17:42,765 --> 00:17:45,059
You've misjudged me.
I've done nothing wrong.

331
00:17:46,561 --> 00:17:48,271
- You truly believe that, don't you?
- Of course I do.

332
00:17:48,396 --> 00:17:50,022
It's just business to you...
all these women dead.

333
00:17:50,147 --> 00:17:52,692
It's just numbers on a spreadsheet
figures on your bottom line.

334
00:17:52,817 --> 00:17:55,403
First of all, I didn't kill anyone.

335
00:17:56,487 --> 00:17:59,115
But perhaps the man who did
wasn't solely concerned with money.

336
00:17:59,282 --> 00:18:01,242
Perhaps he had a greater cause
in mind.

337
00:18:02,076 --> 00:18:03,578
Perhaps the lives of these women
were taken

338
00:18:03,703 --> 00:18:07,331
so that the lives of important men
could be spared...

339
00:18:07,999 --> 00:18:11,669
Scientists, leaders, philanthropists
people who shape the world.

340
00:18:15,214 --> 00:18:17,133
I... I'm not sure how
I'm supposed to react to that.

341
00:18:17,258 --> 00:18:19,176
Oh, come on. It's just us.

342
00:18:19,343 --> 00:18:21,637
If anyone could understand
that perspective, it's you.

343
00:18:21,762 --> 00:18:24,682
Well, you're right. I've worked
with psychopaths and monsters for years

344
00:18:24,807 --> 00:18:27,184
so, yes, I do understand
your perspective...

345
00:18:27,727 --> 00:18:28,936
very well.

346
00:18:30,521 --> 00:18:31,897
Have a lovely afternoon.

347
00:18:34,984 --> 00:18:36,110
Sure.

348
00:18:37,153 --> 00:18:38,237
You too.

349
00:18:43,075 --> 00:18:45,995
We want names
of every organ recipient.

350
00:18:46,120 --> 00:18:48,956
We find these people
we can get them to testify against Ridley.

351
00:18:49,332 --> 00:18:51,334
I, I need another drink.

352
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:53,836
Not this time. Not until you talk.

353
00:18:55,921 --> 00:18:57,048
I... I can't help you.

354
00:18:57,173 --> 00:18:59,383
Ridley was the only one
who knew their names.

355
00:18:59,508 --> 00:19:02,219
He kept the information
on an encrypted laptop.

356
00:19:02,345 --> 00:19:03,804
I never had access to it.

357
00:19:03,929 --> 00:19:06,891
And you never talked to Ridley
about any of these people?

358
00:19:07,266 --> 00:19:08,809
Not once in six years?

359
00:19:11,312 --> 00:19:15,566
Mr. Lark, you have the chance
to do something right for once.

360
00:19:17,318 --> 00:19:18,361
Um...

361
00:19:19,779 --> 00:19:21,906
Uh, well, it's not much, but...

362
00:19:22,740 --> 00:19:24,533
Uh, last week

363
00:19:24,825 --> 00:19:27,703
uh, I had a meeting at, uh,
Ridley's house.

364
00:19:27,828 --> 00:19:32,208
"I got there early
and overheard Ridley with a client."

365
00:19:32,667 --> 00:19:36,337
"He was sick, uh, said he needed
a transplant as soon as possible."

366
00:19:36,545 --> 00:19:38,881
"I heard him say he was Nigerian"

367
00:19:39,006 --> 00:19:42,510
"and in the States conducting meetings
for his oil business."

368
00:19:42,718 --> 00:19:47,348
"H... h... he and Ridley had met
at a fundraiser in Houston."

369
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:48,766
"Okay. Anything else?"

370
00:19:49,225 --> 00:19:52,687
"He had, uh, an expensive
gold watch..."

371
00:19:53,020 --> 00:19:55,231
"A Philippe Badeaux."

372
00:19:56,440 --> 00:19:58,067
I used to have one just like it.

373
00:19:58,859 --> 00:20:02,363
Okay, so you don't have recipient names.

374
00:20:03,322 --> 00:20:04,990
What about Ridley's employee?

375
00:20:05,866 --> 00:20:07,785
You know, anything else
you can tell me about him?

376
00:20:15,710 --> 00:20:16,752
What's this?

377
00:20:16,877 --> 00:20:18,629
Ridley's employee had
that tattooed on his forearm.

378
00:20:18,921 --> 00:20:21,257
Run it through the database.
See if anything pops.

379
00:20:21,382 --> 00:20:22,425
You got it.

380
00:21:25,446 --> 00:21:26,489
Jane.

381
00:21:34,705 --> 00:21:35,956
Scooch over.

382
00:21:43,714 --> 00:21:45,925
How often do you sleep
in your own bed, anyway?

383
00:21:46,050 --> 00:21:48,719
Oh, I don't like that bed.
Too many lumps.

384
00:21:48,844 --> 00:21:50,346
Maybe you should get a new bed.

385
00:21:53,849 --> 00:21:55,309
Actually, you know what?

386
00:21:56,310 --> 00:21:58,145
I always did like this couch.

387
00:22:01,607 --> 00:22:02,650
Jane.

388
00:22:08,739 --> 00:22:09,782
Jane...

389
00:22:10,908 --> 00:22:12,284
Take your call, Lisbon.

390
00:22:20,626 --> 00:22:21,669
Fischer?

391
00:22:24,672 --> 00:22:25,714
What? When?

392
00:22:32,471 --> 00:22:34,139
Guards found him this morning.

393
00:22:34,306 --> 00:22:36,100
- Did he leave a note?
- No.

394
00:22:36,517 --> 00:22:38,102
But they found that on his bed.

395
00:22:39,728 --> 00:22:41,146
Now, what do you make of that?

396
00:22:57,244 --> 00:22:58,537
Does this belong to you?

397
00:23:02,583 --> 00:23:03,834
Where did you find this?

398
00:23:04,084 --> 00:23:05,794
We found it in your father's cell.

399
00:23:06,503 --> 00:23:08,213
But that's impossible.

400
00:23:09,089 --> 00:23:12,092
I was wearing it last night.
I... I thought I lost it.

401
00:23:13,844 --> 00:23:14,887
April...

402
00:23:16,305 --> 00:23:17,639
Were you alone last night?

403
00:23:18,682 --> 00:23:19,725
Yes.

404
00:23:20,058 --> 00:23:21,101
Why?

405
00:23:22,978 --> 00:23:26,064
Wait, a... are you telling me
that there was someone in my house?

406
00:23:30,235 --> 00:23:31,278
Oh, my God.

407
00:23:33,322 --> 00:23:36,325
Ridley found her.
That's why Lark killed himself.

408
00:23:36,450 --> 00:23:38,785
And killed our case
against Ridley in the process.

409
00:23:38,911 --> 00:23:40,495
Maybe so, but we can't prove it.

410
00:23:40,621 --> 00:23:42,831
I'm having Wylie pull
the security tapes from the jail.

411
00:23:42,956 --> 00:23:46,001
Maybe we can find out how Ridley
got the necklace into Lark's cell.

412
00:23:46,126 --> 00:23:48,128
Good idea.
What about the man that worked for him?

413
00:23:48,253 --> 00:23:51,757
We're looking into it but all we have
is a vague description and a tattoo.

414
00:23:52,049 --> 00:23:53,717
- The Nigerian.
- Excuse me?

415
00:23:53,842 --> 00:23:56,261
The man Lark spoke of.
The man Ridley met with.

416
00:23:56,511 --> 00:23:59,056
If Lark was right and the man
was an organ recipient

417
00:23:59,181 --> 00:24:00,724
he'd be able to implicate Ridley.

418
00:24:00,891 --> 00:24:03,727
If he'll talk
and if he actually was a recipient.

419
00:24:03,852 --> 00:24:05,270
We don't even know
if he's still in the country.

420
00:24:05,479 --> 00:24:07,022
You don't have to be so negative.

421
00:24:08,815 --> 00:24:10,067
You haven't found her?

422
00:24:11,151 --> 00:24:12,277
No. Not yet.

423
00:24:12,611 --> 00:24:13,946
She's alive.

424
00:24:14,613 --> 00:24:15,822
I know she is.

425
00:24:16,657 --> 00:24:18,033
We think we've narrowed down
her location

426
00:24:18,158 --> 00:24:20,410
to one of four cargo ships
headed for Colombia.

427
00:24:21,536 --> 00:24:22,579
If we're right

428
00:24:23,080 --> 00:24:24,623
she should be arriving
within 12 hours

429
00:24:24,748 --> 00:24:26,124
and we'll be there to find her.

430
00:24:27,626 --> 00:24:28,835
And if you're wrong?

431
00:25:02,828 --> 00:25:04,955
I got him.
Well not him, specifically

432
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:07,833
but I think I found the fundraising event
Lark was talking about.

433
00:25:08,625 --> 00:25:13,005
The Blue Skies gala.
It was held six weeks ago in Houston.

434
00:25:13,213 --> 00:25:15,090
Looks like it was
an environmental charity event

435
00:25:15,215 --> 00:25:17,175
put on by domestic oil companies.

436
00:25:17,300 --> 00:25:21,096
Including representatives
from Africa-based Ar Ahim Energy.

437
00:25:21,555 --> 00:25:24,307
This must be the event
where Ridley met this Nigerian man.

438
00:25:24,474 --> 00:25:25,517
Nice work.

439
00:25:25,642 --> 00:25:27,561
E-mail me the contact info
for the event company.

440
00:25:27,728 --> 00:25:28,770
Already did.

441
00:25:33,942 --> 00:25:35,277
Hi. I'm Patrick Jane.

442
00:25:35,402 --> 00:25:38,030
I'd like to see the evidence
from the abandoned hospital...

443
00:25:38,155 --> 00:25:39,406
All of it, please.

444
00:25:49,416 --> 00:25:51,543
So, these are all the pictures
from the Blue Skies gala?

445
00:25:51,668 --> 00:25:54,337
Every last one according
to the event-planning company.

446
00:25:54,796 --> 00:25:56,882
The Nigerian man is here somewhere.

447
00:25:57,257 --> 00:25:59,092
You know, we can wait
for Jane if you want.

448
00:25:59,259 --> 00:26:01,053
Why? He hates this kinda stuff.

449
00:26:01,219 --> 00:26:02,804
I don't know.
You guys always work together.

450
00:26:03,138 --> 00:26:04,181
Not always.

451
00:26:09,019 --> 00:26:10,645
Here's a possibility.

452
00:26:11,730 --> 00:26:14,024
Lark said the man wore
an expensive watch.

453
00:26:14,149 --> 00:26:16,151
- Gold, right?
- That's him.

454
00:26:16,318 --> 00:26:18,361
His name is Jonas Diop. You were right.

455
00:26:18,487 --> 00:26:20,113
He's a Nigerian oil executive.

456
00:26:20,238 --> 00:26:22,574
But according
to his recent credit-card activity

457
00:26:22,699 --> 00:26:23,909
he's still in the Houston Area.

458
00:26:24,076 --> 00:26:25,285
Do you know where he's staying?

459
00:26:25,619 --> 00:26:27,662
Uh...

460
00:26:27,788 --> 00:26:28,830
The Park Huntley hotel.

461
00:26:43,261 --> 00:26:45,680
- Hello?
- "Mr. Diop, hello."

462
00:26:46,640 --> 00:26:49,017
How are you?
You doing okay?

463
00:26:50,936 --> 00:26:54,314
I hate to have to make this call
but the FBI may be looking for you.

464
00:26:57,109 --> 00:26:59,194
You knew the risks
when you got involved.

465
00:26:59,444 --> 00:27:01,238
If I were you, I'd leave
the country immediately.

466
00:27:09,329 --> 00:27:12,415
- Lisbon?
- "I'm afraid I've got some bad news, Jane."

467
00:27:12,999 --> 00:27:15,335
- "What's up?"
- Jonas Diop's gone.

468
00:27:15,460 --> 00:27:17,420
His driver says he left
the Houston airport

469
00:27:17,546 --> 00:27:19,214
on a private plane 20 minutes ago.

470
00:27:19,381 --> 00:27:20,757
"He's headed back to Nigeria."

471
00:27:21,133 --> 00:27:23,051
- Ridley.
- "He was our only lead."

472
00:27:23,176 --> 00:27:24,719
I'm running out of ideas.

473
00:27:25,053 --> 00:27:26,096
Well, I'm not.

474
00:27:26,388 --> 00:27:28,765
- You have a plan?
- Yeah, but I'm gonna need your help.

475
00:27:29,099 --> 00:27:30,767
Why don't I like the sound of that?

476
00:27:31,268 --> 00:27:33,228
Well, I'm gonna need you
to be deceitful and corrupt

477
00:27:33,353 --> 00:27:35,105
and you'll be breaking
numerous laws.

478
00:27:37,190 --> 00:27:38,233
Lisbon?

479
00:27:39,276 --> 00:27:41,611
- I'm thinking.
- Ridley will walk.

480
00:27:41,736 --> 00:27:45,365
This is the only way.
Hey, hey, are you a medium or a small?

481
00:27:45,490 --> 00:27:47,200
You're not helping yourself
out here, Jane.

482
00:27:47,534 --> 00:27:49,828
Meet me at Wheatondale
in two hours.

483
00:28:01,298 --> 00:28:02,340
Thank you.

484
00:28:11,725 --> 00:28:14,144
- What is this?
- You'd guess scotch, wouldn't you?

485
00:28:15,270 --> 00:28:18,315
Japanese whiskey...
25-year-old single malt.

486
00:28:19,441 --> 00:28:20,984
One of my few indulgences.

487
00:28:22,152 --> 00:28:24,237
It's delicious.
But that's just scotch.

488
00:28:24,362 --> 00:28:26,615
- It's Speyside. I'm sure of it.
- Here.

489
00:28:27,866 --> 00:28:29,075
I'll prove it to you.

490
00:28:41,463 --> 00:28:44,841
Wow. Look at that.
I stand corrected.

491
00:28:46,801 --> 00:28:48,637
You're not the first one
to make that mistake.

492
00:28:49,679 --> 00:28:52,265
People assume it's not as good,
but they're wrong.

493
00:28:52,557 --> 00:28:53,850
It's better, in fact.

494
00:28:54,476 --> 00:28:57,979
Some say it's the water,
but I have my own theory.

495
00:28:58,772 --> 00:29:00,065
I think it's cultural.

496
00:29:01,191 --> 00:29:03,401
Japanese have it in their blood

497
00:29:03,526 --> 00:29:06,154
to sacrifice everything
in order to succeed...

498
00:29:06,947 --> 00:29:08,073
Like I do.

499
00:29:09,199 --> 00:29:10,533
Like you do, I believe.

500
00:29:13,203 --> 00:29:16,331
We tracked down one of your clients...
Jonas Diop.

501
00:29:17,165 --> 00:29:20,043
Diop's a business associate,
not a client.

502
00:29:20,377 --> 00:29:22,337
But I'm curious...
what did he have to say?

503
00:29:22,504 --> 00:29:24,130
Nothing.
He fled the country

504
00:29:24,256 --> 00:29:26,299
as soon as he heard we wanted
to talk to him.

505
00:29:26,967 --> 00:29:28,009
How unfortunate.

506
00:29:28,134 --> 00:29:29,970
Yeah, I know it was you
that tipped him off.

507
00:29:30,512 --> 00:29:33,974
So far, you've been one step
ahead of me but, uh...

508
00:29:34,182 --> 00:29:36,101
Well, sooner or later...

509
00:29:36,268 --> 00:29:38,812
Oh, if you and the FBI were gonna
prove your case against me

510
00:29:38,937 --> 00:29:40,647
you would've done so by now.

511
00:29:41,690 --> 00:29:43,692
No, I think you've lost
the battle, my friend.

512
00:29:44,609 --> 00:29:47,779
Best to concede defeat and move on.

513
00:29:53,034 --> 00:29:54,661
I'm not sure that I can do that.

514
00:30:00,125 --> 00:30:01,418
Something wrong?

515
00:30:06,047 --> 00:30:07,966
I, uh...

516
00:30:10,135 --> 00:30:12,387
I... I don't feel well.

517
00:30:16,933 --> 00:30:18,476
I think it's time for you to leave.

518
00:30:20,645 --> 00:30:21,688
Yeah, might be time.

519
00:30:21,813 --> 00:30:24,107
Y... you don't look so good...
a little pale.

520
00:30:25,108 --> 00:30:26,151
Shaky.

521
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:29,154
You look like you need to lie down.

522
00:30:31,865 --> 00:30:34,743
It's almost as though
you've been... drugged.

523
00:30:35,994 --> 00:30:39,122
You know, I've been thinking
about what you said, you know?

524
00:30:39,247 --> 00:30:42,584
Some lives are more valuable
than others.

525
00:30:44,794 --> 00:30:45,837
Anyway...

526
00:30:48,465 --> 00:30:50,091
I figured, uh...

527
00:30:51,259 --> 00:30:52,719
Well, I couldn't agree more.

528
00:30:54,304 --> 00:30:55,680
Sorry you didn't catch it.

529
00:30:56,598 --> 00:30:57,849
"Sayonara."

530
00:31:22,121 --> 00:31:24,290
- All set?
- Yeah.

531
00:31:24,999 --> 00:31:27,168
Jane, we don't have to do this.
We can still stop.

532
00:31:27,293 --> 00:31:29,754
No, no. It's a little late
for that, isn't it?

533
00:31:33,758 --> 00:31:35,259
- Is he restrained?
- Yes.

534
00:31:37,970 --> 00:31:40,431
And the gag is securely
in his mouth?

535
00:31:40,723 --> 00:31:42,475
- Yes, it is.
- Okay.

536
00:32:01,327 --> 00:32:02,912
Don't worry. This won't hurt a bit.

537
00:32:05,164 --> 00:32:06,499
He's a screamer.

538
00:32:08,126 --> 00:32:09,168
Hold him.

539
00:32:13,256 --> 00:32:15,967
- Lisbon, hold him still.
- I'm trying, Jane.

540
00:32:16,300 --> 00:32:17,343
No!

541
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:18,886
He's not gonna last long.

542
00:32:19,220 --> 00:32:20,513
That's all right. He isn't meant to.

543
00:32:21,097 --> 00:32:22,306
Sponge.

544
00:32:28,771 --> 00:32:30,022
Sponge.

545
00:32:32,108 --> 00:32:33,234
Get it in there.

546
00:32:37,363 --> 00:32:38,406
Oh my God!

547
00:32:58,050 --> 00:32:59,260
He's gone.

548
00:33:02,722 --> 00:33:03,764
He's awake.

549
00:33:03,890 --> 00:33:05,975
You said he was gonna be
unconscious, Jane!

550
00:33:06,100 --> 00:33:07,852
It doesn't matter what I said.

551
00:33:08,394 --> 00:33:09,645
What's going on?

552
00:33:10,188 --> 00:33:11,314
Where am I?

553
00:33:11,522 --> 00:33:12,607
Quiet.

554
00:33:21,115 --> 00:33:22,325
You killed him.

555
00:33:23,451 --> 00:33:25,828
- You killed Anthony.
- I said, be quiet.

556
00:33:26,245 --> 00:33:27,455
What are you gonna do to me?

557
00:33:27,663 --> 00:33:29,248
I think you already know
the answer to that.

558
00:33:29,373 --> 00:33:30,541
You're insane!

559
00:33:30,958 --> 00:33:32,960
- Let me go now!
- Or else what?

560
00:33:35,421 --> 00:33:38,549
You're a cop. You can't do this.
This is wrong.

561
00:33:38,716 --> 00:33:42,178
Not after what you've done.
This is poetic justice.

562
00:33:42,553 --> 00:33:44,138
What do you want, a confession?

563
00:33:44,263 --> 00:33:45,723
You know it'll never hold up in court.

564
00:33:46,182 --> 00:33:48,017
Eh, I think you might be right
about that.

565
00:33:48,392 --> 00:33:51,145
Stop. Stop!
I'll tell you everything!

566
00:33:51,270 --> 00:33:54,232
- I think it's a bit late for that.
- Just, would you, wait. Wait.

567
00:33:54,774 --> 00:33:56,859
My laptop. I...

568
00:33:57,068 --> 00:33:59,195
I kept records... of everyone.

569
00:33:59,320 --> 00:34:02,323
We know about the laptop.
It's password-protected.

570
00:34:02,865 --> 00:34:04,992
I'll tell you the passwords! Stop!

571
00:34:05,117 --> 00:34:07,203
Stop. I'll tell you anything you want.

572
00:34:07,411 --> 00:34:10,373
Just stop, please.
Please.

573
00:34:12,708 --> 00:34:14,961
Tell it to me. What's the password?

574
00:34:16,671 --> 00:34:18,506
I'm the last person
who can save you.

575
00:34:20,925 --> 00:34:23,511
F79VR22.

576
00:34:24,845 --> 00:34:25,930
All right?

577
00:34:37,525 --> 00:34:39,277
We got it. We're done here.

578
00:34:49,203 --> 00:34:51,080
Your agents tortured my client.

579
00:34:51,205 --> 00:34:52,623
- He's lying.
- Jane.

580
00:34:54,083 --> 00:34:56,002
There is no evidence
of physical abuse.

581
00:34:56,127 --> 00:34:58,546
Psychological torture
is still torture, Agent Abbott.

582
00:34:58,921 --> 00:35:01,757
My client was made to believe
that he'd be killed by Mr. Jane

583
00:35:02,049 --> 00:35:04,343
making any evidence
you may have gathered inadmissible.

584
00:35:04,468 --> 00:35:07,763
Yes, if that evidence is ruled
to have been gathered illegally.

585
00:35:07,930 --> 00:35:09,056
Which it won't be.

586
00:35:09,181 --> 00:35:11,225
My client has sworn
to these accusations.

587
00:35:11,350 --> 00:35:14,562
Your client is a monster facing life
in prison for what he's done.

588
00:35:14,687 --> 00:35:16,606
I'd say just about anything
if I were him.

589
00:35:19,734 --> 00:35:20,776
You killed him.

590
00:35:21,319 --> 00:35:22,403
Please, don't say anything.

591
00:35:22,528 --> 00:35:25,031
He murdered a man that worked for me.
Anthony Tremmel.

592
00:35:25,698 --> 00:35:27,533
- You're certain?
- I watched him die.

593
00:35:31,912 --> 00:35:33,998
This is from a live security feed...

594
00:35:35,291 --> 00:35:37,293
from our holding cells downstairs.

595
00:35:46,761 --> 00:35:48,262
No...
It... it can't...

596
00:35:49,430 --> 00:35:50,765
I saw him.

597
00:35:52,016 --> 00:35:54,810
Perhaps your client
would like to revise his statement.

598
00:36:02,193 --> 00:36:03,235
Hey.

599
00:36:03,986 --> 00:36:05,029
Try this.

600
00:36:07,990 --> 00:36:09,033
How you doing?

601
00:36:09,367 --> 00:36:11,661
Good. Are you kidding?
That was amazing.

602
00:36:12,578 --> 00:36:13,621
Can we do it again?

603
00:36:15,039 --> 00:36:18,209
Lisbon. My office. Now.

604
00:36:26,801 --> 00:36:28,552
Mr. Ridley and his lawyer

605
00:36:28,678 --> 00:36:32,890
have made some serious claims
against you two.

606
00:36:33,015 --> 00:36:35,017
They're only serious if they're true.

607
00:36:36,352 --> 00:36:37,395
Are they?

608
00:36:38,771 --> 00:36:39,814
No.

609
00:36:42,650 --> 00:36:45,277
- No.
- So, you didn't threaten to kill Ridley?

610
00:36:45,569 --> 00:36:47,822
And you didn't stage the death
of his associate?

611
00:36:48,114 --> 00:36:49,699
No, of course not.

612
00:36:51,575 --> 00:36:53,619
All right.
Well, then I'm done with you.

613
00:36:53,744 --> 00:36:55,663
- Okay.
- Not you, Lisbon.

614
00:37:04,338 --> 00:37:05,423
Jane is a liar.

615
00:37:05,673 --> 00:37:08,092
No need to deny it. I expect it of him.

616
00:37:08,467 --> 00:37:10,052
But you're not a liar, Lisbon.

617
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:13,973
You're an honest, good person
with a long career ahead of you

618
00:37:14,432 --> 00:37:18,394
and I don't wanna see you throw away
this new opportunity of yours.

619
00:37:18,978 --> 00:37:20,020
So...

620
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:22,565
I'm gonna ask you one more time.

621
00:37:23,941 --> 00:37:27,528
Is there any truth
to Ridley's accusations?

622
00:37:29,739 --> 00:37:33,033
No.
Everything Jane said was true.

623
00:37:43,586 --> 00:37:44,920
You had an update for me?

624
00:37:46,338 --> 00:37:47,381
I do.

625
00:37:48,883 --> 00:37:51,302
"Earlier today, FBI agents working
with Colombian authorities"

626
00:37:51,427 --> 00:37:53,804
"searched a container ship
in San Marta."

627
00:37:54,513 --> 00:37:56,223
"They found 30 women on board."

628
00:37:56,557 --> 00:37:58,267
"Your sister, Amy, was one of them."

629
00:38:12,531 --> 00:38:13,699
"We found her."

630
00:38:42,853 --> 00:38:44,522
- There you are.
- Hey.

631
00:38:44,647 --> 00:38:47,316
- Ready to go?
- I am so ready.

632
00:38:47,441 --> 00:38:49,318
You won't believe the day
I've had.

633
00:38:49,443 --> 00:38:50,486
Yeah?

634
00:38:51,987 --> 00:38:54,615
Look, uh, about D.C.

635
00:38:56,700 --> 00:38:59,370
I know it's a lot to take in
and I didn't mean to pressure you.

636
00:38:59,620 --> 00:39:00,996
Yeah, you did.

637
00:39:01,747 --> 00:39:04,166
Yeah, I did.
Maybe a little.

638
00:39:05,376 --> 00:39:09,922
But it's your life
and I just wanna be a part of it.

639
00:39:10,673 --> 00:39:12,132
I've made up my mind.

640
00:39:12,925 --> 00:39:15,594
I'm gonna call Don tomorrow
and tell him I'll take the job.

641
00:39:16,095 --> 00:39:18,347
- Are you sure?
- Yeah, I'm sure.

642
00:39:22,852 --> 00:39:23,894
Wow.

643
00:39:27,147 --> 00:39:29,733
Wow. I wish we were somewhere
more romantic

644
00:39:29,900 --> 00:39:31,443
but what the hell?

645
00:39:32,778 --> 00:39:34,196
Will you marry me?

646
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:37,283
What?

647
00:39:38,200 --> 00:39:40,119
- What?
- Don't freak out.

648
00:39:40,619 --> 00:39:42,788
Um, wow. Marry you?

649
00:39:43,664 --> 00:39:45,332
- Um...
- I know. I know.

650
00:39:45,457 --> 00:39:48,210
- You need time to think about it.
- Yeah. It's, it's a big decision.

651
00:39:48,335 --> 00:39:49,503
It's huge.

652
00:39:51,338 --> 00:39:53,841
There's no pressure, okay?

653
00:39:54,091 --> 00:39:55,134
Okay.

654
00:39:58,888 --> 00:40:00,306
You're a tough date.

655
00:40:02,975 --> 00:40:04,810
Have you told Jane
that you're leaving yet?

656
00:40:05,019 --> 00:40:06,604
No. No, not yet.

657
00:40:08,063 --> 00:40:10,232
- He'll understand.
- Yeah.

658
00:40:11,150 --> 00:40:12,192
All right.

659
00:40:13,527 --> 00:40:15,237
- I'll be downstairs.
- All right.

660
00:40:35,591 --> 00:40:37,009
How'd it go with Abbott?

661
00:40:41,513 --> 00:40:42,556
Thanks.

662
00:40:46,727 --> 00:40:48,812
We make a good team sometimes.

663
00:40:49,688 --> 00:40:51,398
Yeah. We do.

664
00:40:54,735 --> 00:40:55,819
You heading out?

665
00:40:57,071 --> 00:40:58,948
Yeah.
I... I'll see you tomorrow?

666
00:41:00,950 --> 00:41:02,117
I'll be here.

667
00:41:08,040 --> 00:41:09,124
Good night.

